\define greeting(who:"world")
Hello, $who$! Also: $(currentTiddler)$ and ${ [tag[Task]] }$.
\end

\procedure greeting-proc(who:"world")
Hello, <<who>>! A procedure body reads $who$ as literal text, never a substitution.
\end

\function double(n)
<n> +[multiply[2]]
\end

\widget $my.greeting(who:"world")
<p>Hello, <<who>>!</p>
\end

\define $dollar.macro() one line
\procedure $dollar.proc() one line
\function $dollar.fn() [tag[Task]]
\widget $dollar.widget() one line

\define default-forms(a:"double", b:'single', c:"""triple""", d:unquoted)
$a$ $b$ $c$ $d$
\end

\procedure default-forms-proc(a:"double", b:'single', c:"""triple""", d:unquoted)
A procedure's own default value opens the same four forms a `\define`'s does — double, single and
triple quoted, and bare — but never substitutes into its own body the way `$a$` would in a
`\define`. This body reads <<a>> <<b>> <<c>> <<d>> as ordinary calls instead, each one naming a
parameter that stands as a variable in its own right, the way any `\procedure` parameter does.
\end

DEFINITIONS SIDE BY SIDE, called immediately after standing. `\define` still builds a MACRO —
macrodef.js, with `$param$` substitution a `\procedure` lacks — while `\procedure`, `\function` and
`\widget` all build the same PROCEDURE-family thing a `<<x>>` or `$variable` call resolves. The
grammar keeps exactly that distinction: a `\define` name and body read `entity.name.variable.macro`
/ `meta.variable.macro.body`, never `.procedure` — every OTHER definition above reads
`entity.name.variable.procedure` / `entity.name.variable.function` / `entity.name.variable.widget`,
the call-side family a `<<x>>` invocation shares since TiddlyWiki 5.3.

<<greeting>>

<<greeting-proc>>

<$transclude $variable="double" n="21"/>

<$my.greeting/>

Every one of the four kinds keeps a dollar in its name the same way: `\define $dollar.macro()`,
`\procedure $dollar.proc()`, `\function $dollar.fn()` and `\widget $dollar.widget()` above all
parsed — fnprocdef.js and macrodef.js both read `[^(\s]+` for a name, admitting the dollar either
way.
